    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70057</id>
    <title>Value creation and capture in artificial intelligence‐enabled ecosystems</title>
    <updated>2026-10-13T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Rahul Kapoor, Manav Raj</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We develop an ecosystem perspective on AI‐enabled value creation, grounded in AI's characteristics as a general‐purpose technology. We consider that AI can support multiple value propositions realized through distinct but interconnected ecosystems. Within this structure, AI innovators face choices concerning which value propositions to support, whether to pursue them through general‐purpose or specialized offers, and whether to make those offers open or closed. Users, in turn, are strategic actors facing choices among alternative ecosystem configurations of focal offers and complements through which the same value proposition might be realized. These choices by innovators and users may alter ecosystem roles and competitive relationships over time. We conclude by outlining research opportunities for strategy and innovation scholars examining how firms create and capture value in AI‐enabled ecosystems.Managerial SummaryValue creation with AI depends not only on the technology's capability but on the actors whose combined contributions deliver a value proposition. AI supports many value propositions, each through its own ecosystem, with some elements shared across them. AI innovators choose which value propositions to serve, whether to do so through general‐purpose or specialized offers, and whether to make their offers open or proprietary. User firms choose among alternative configurations of offers and complements through which the same value proposition can be realized. These choices may shift the competitive relationships between actors within an ecosystem over time. This perspective surfaces questions of relevance for scholars and practitioners regarding value creation and capture with AI.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/sej.70057"/>
    <category term="Strategic Entrepreneurship Journal"/>
    <published>2026-10-13T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2025.21133</id>
    <title>The Paradox of Inventors’ Job Security</title>
    <updated>2026-10-13T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>David Wehrheim, Eduardo Melero, Neus Palomeras</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Organization Science&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper investigates how job security affects the types of innovations generated by corporate inventors. By increasing inventors’ prospects of remaining with their current employer, job security strengthens their incentives to invest in firm-specific technological knowledge. Thus, whereas employment protection provides inventors with a safety net to fail that could facilitate exploration, it instead drives them to produce innovations that draw more heavily on internal technologies and are less novel. We test this argument using matched employer–employee data constructed from patent records. To examine inventor-level responses to increased job security, we exploit the staggered adoption of wrongful discharge laws across U.S. states between 1980 and 1999. Using cross-state variation over time in a difference-in-differences framework, we find that increased job security leads inventors to produce innovations that rely more intensely on their firms’ technologies, are less novel, and generate higher private value to their employers.Funding: This work was supported by Grants PID2021-126435NB-C21 and PID2021-126153NB-I00, both funded by the Spanish Ministry of Science and Innovation (MCIN) / State Research Agency (AEI) / 10.13039/501100011033 and by the European Regional Development Fund (ERDF) “A Way of Making Europe,” and by Grant PID2024-155952NB-I00, funded by the Spanish Ministry of Science, Innovation and Universities (MICIU) / AEI / 10.13039/501100011033 and by the ERDF / European Union (EU)”.Supplemental Material: The online appendix is available at https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2025.21133 .</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/orsc.2025.21133"/>
    <category term="Organization Science"/>
    <published>2026-10-13T00:00:00+00:00</published>
