    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1200</id>
    <title>Seeing Less, Engaging More: Rethinking Early User Experience on Generative AI Cocreation Platforms—Findings from a Field Experiment</title>
    <updated>2026-10-20T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Shenyang Jiang, Akshat Lakhiwal, Che-Wei Liu, Jiang Duan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) content generation platforms such as DALL·E or Midjourney produce impressive content in seconds. But this very immediacy often flattens creation into a single moment, stripping away the evolving experience shown to sustain engagement. In response, platforms have increasingly been experimenting with how much output to reveal and how to prompt registration, restoring some of that lost experience. How, then, should early user experience be structured to bolster engagement? This research studies two user experience levers. The first is fulfillment, that is, the extent to which GenAI content is revealed before registration. The second is the framing of the registration message itself. We partnered with a GenAI content generation platform, running a randomized field experiment with over 20,000 first-time users. Full fulfillment satisfied users immediately, but partial fulfillment worked better, activating two motivational pathways: value-in-use as the output reflected the user’s prompt and curiosity about what remained unseen. Counterintuitively, loss-framed registration messages substituted for rather than reinforced this effect, increasing registration under partial disclosure but losing impact once the full output was visible. These effects extended beyond registration, as users returned more often and engaged more substantively. Revealing less, structured well, is what turns a fleeting encounter into lasting engagement.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1200"/>
    <category term="Information Systems Research"/>
    <published>2026-10-20T00:00:00+00:00</published>
