  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag119</id>
    <title>Price Gouging and the Monopoly Option</title>
    <updated>2026-10-26T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Samuel Häfner, Curtis R Taylor</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Economic Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We investigate a dynamic oligopoly model with two phases: a normal phase where an essential input is available, and a disruption phase where firms must rely on inventories. Disruptions create an overcutting pricing incentive arising from the option to forgo sales and monopolize the market once rivals stock out. This monopoly option causes the price to jump to a supracompetitive level at the advent of the disruption and to remain above the competitive price path throughout. Price caps raise welfare, but reduce inventory investment. Government reserves reduce prices and raise buffer stocks, as do reserve mandates when financially feasible.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag119"/>
    <category term="Review of Economic Studies"/>
    <published>2026-10-26T00:00:00+00:00</published>
