    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1330</id>
    <title>Enhancing AI Use: How Complementary System Information Drives Delegation Frequency and Effectiveness</title>
    <updated>2026-10-28T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Anna Taudien, Andreas Fügener, Alok Gupta, Wolfgang Ketter</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Organizations increasingly deploy AI that works alongside employees rather than replacing them. In settings such as visual inspection, content moderation, or applicant screening, employees decide task by task whether to do the work themselves or delegate it to AI, yet they often delegate suboptimally, leaving much of the promised benefit of AI unrealized. Our research yields a design recommendation for AI systems: tell users how certain the AI is before a decision and whether its decisions turn out to be correct afterwards. Each type of information alone had no positive effect, and sometimes even reduced effective delegation. Combined, however, they increased delegation and helped users direct AI to the tasks where it had a performance advantage. The combination led to human-AI teams outperforming both AI alone and humans alone, showing that humans still add value when AI enters decision making. This matters for organizations where employees retain decision rights for the use of AI.</content>
